= Computation — Technical =

== Abstract computation ==
An abstract computation may be represented as a sequence or branching structure of configurations connected by a transition relation.

A generic transition system can be written as:

<pre>
(S, I, O, →)
</pre>

where <code>S</code> is a state/configuration space, <code>I</code> and <code>O</code> characterize inputs and outputs where applicable, and <code>→</code> is an allowed transition relation.

Different models make different primitives available and therefore support different analyses of computability and resource cost.

== Classical models ==
Important models include:
* Turing machines;
* lambda calculus;
* recursive functions;
* finite-state machines;
* random-access machines;
* register machines;
* circuits.

The equivalence of several classical models with respect to effectively calculable functions motivated the Church–Turing thesis.

== Church–Turing thesis ==
The Church–Turing thesis identifies the informal notion of effective calculability with Turing-computable (equivalently, classically computable) functions.

It is not a theorem that follows solely from the definition of a Turing machine because one side of the equivalence is an informal pre-formal concept. Stronger claims about what can be computed by physically possible devices are variants of a physical Church–Turing thesis and require additional empirical assumptions.

== Computability and complexity ==
[[Computability]] classifies what can be computed in principle under a model.

[[Computational complexity]] studies the resources required. Complexity classes therefore presuppose both a problem representation and a computational resource model.

== Nondeterministic, probabilistic, and quantum models ==
A nondeterministic transition relation permits multiple possible successor configurations. Probabilistic models associate probabilities with transitions or choices. Quantum computation replaces classical state evolution with quantum-state operations and measurement.

These models must be distinguished from implementation noise or ordinary uncertainty about a deterministic system.

== Interactive and distributed computation ==
Interactive and distributed systems do not fit neatly into a single closed input-to-output function. Their observable behavior may depend on:
* message order;
* external events;
* timing;
* failures;
* concurrency;
* other agents.

Behavioral equivalence, traces, transition systems, process calculi, and distributed-computing models are therefore often more useful than a single mathematical function.

== Concrete computation ==
A physical device implements an abstract computational description only under some mapping between physical states/processes and formal states/operations. Philosophical accounts differ on what makes such an implementation nontrivial rather than an arbitrary interpretation.

This is why "can be computationally modeled" should not be treated as equivalent to "is literally computing."

